// this is a statically CONFIGURED merge
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Same shape as det_merge, but the selection is not driven by a SEL channel at
// run time -- it is driven by config BITS that are set once (think FPGA-style
// configuration SRAM, or a route programmed into a switch and then left alone).
// The bits are plain bools, not channels, so there is no handshake on the
// control path at all. Against det_merge that is the whole difference, and
// against non_det_merge it is that plus the arbiter.
//
// CONTRACT: cfg_in must be ONE-HOT. It is NOT checked in hardware.
//   Zero bits set -> the select has no true guard and the process deadlocks.
//   Two bits set  -> two true guards in a deterministic [] select, which is an
//                    error, not an arbitration. This is exactly the difference
//                    from non_det_merge: that one probes and pays for an
//                    arbiter, this one trusts the configuration.
//
// Sweep knobs: width, inDim.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

template <pint width; pint inDim> defproc config_merge(
    chan?(int<width>) IN[inDim];
    bool? cfg_in[inDim];
    chan!(int<width>) OUT
    ){
        int<width> v;
        chp{
        *[
            [ ([] i : inDim : cfg_in[i] -> IN[i]?v ) ];
            OUT!v
        ]
        }
}
